# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliveroo sandbox client_secret — the decision the header above left open.
#
# The two rules added in #315 (`deliveroo-client-secret-shape` and
# `-context`, see .gitleaks.toml) finally detect what the header said was
# "simply never detected". Four findings, one commit, two files, two rules
# each — the full list, measured on the run of 2026-09-04 over 466 commits:
#
#   7cf4d41  apps/restaurant-theme/e2e/deliveroo/test-config.ts:16   (both rules)
#   7cf4d41  scripts/deliveroo-menu-scenarios.sh:10                  (both rules)
#
# This entry is NOT the JWT case above, and must not be read as it. That token
# was accepted because it was provably dead: expired, 15-minute lifetime,
# localhost scope. This secret is recorded by
# tasks/secret-rotation-runbook.md as **still unrotated**, and #172 (A.1 —
# regenerate in the Deliveroo Developer Portal) is still open. Nothing here
# makes it safe.
#
# What it is accepted for is narrower, and worth stating exactly: since #315
# landed, `security.yml` has failed on `main` itself on every run. A check that
# is red on every commit reports the same four known findings for ever, so a
# NEW leak arrives as one more line in a list nobody reads. Silencing four
# known findings is what lets the scanner speak again about a fifth.
#
# Verified before accepting, on 2026-09-04:
#   - `apps/restaurant-theme/e2e/deliveroo/test-config.ts` no longer exists at
#     HEAD; the app was renamed to `apps/reference`.
#   - `scripts/deliveroo-menu-scenarios.sh` still exists and is CLEAN: it reads
#     `${DELIVEROO_CLIENT_SECRET:-}` with an empty default, under an explicit
#     "NEVER hardcode secrets here". The working tree carries no secret.
#   - Both findings are therefore historical only, on one commit of
#     2026-03-11, already inventoried by the runbook.
#
# Scoped per fingerprint — commit, file, rule and line — deliberately. Not by
# file, not by rule: a NEW secret in either file, or on another line, still
# fails the scan.
#
# That last sentence was an assertion until 2026-09-09; it is now a measurement.
# Reproduced with the CI-pinned gitleaks 8.21.2 against a throwaway repo holding
# a FABRICATED 52-char base36 token in these same two paths and the same two
# syntactic positions (`:-` in a shell default, `|| "` in TypeScript):
#
#   no .gitleaksignore                        -> leaks found: 4    exit 1
#   these four fingerprints present           -> no leaks found    exit 0
#   + a fifth secret, new line, same file     -> leaks found: 1    exit 1
#
# One, not five and not zero. The scoping does what it says.
#
# Remove these four lines when Part B rewrites the history. They are the
# scanner's memory of a leak that has not been cleaned, not a sign it was.
